# PushUpGame — Cursor Project Rules

## Swift & concurrency
- Swift Concurrency only: async/await and actors. Do not introduce Combine unless
  explicitly asked. The one exception is AVFoundation's inherently delegate-based
  camera callback API — bridge it into AsyncStream at the boundary, don't let delegate
  patterns leak further into the app.
- Mark UI-facing types @MainActor explicitly rather than relying on inferred isolation.
- Prefer actors for shared mutable state accessed from multiple execution contexts
  (camera callbacks + UI reads). Do not make purely synchronous, single-caller logic
  (e.g. PushUpAnalyzer, PushUpStateMachine, Geometry) into actors — that adds needless
  await-hopping for no thread-safety benefit.

## Architecture boundaries
- Vision may only be imported inside the Vision/ folder.
- AVFoundation may only be imported inside the Camera/ folder.
- SwiftUI/UIKit may only be imported inside Features/*/Views/, App/, and UI/.
- Domain/ must have zero imports beyond Foundation and CoreGraphics. If a change would
  require importing anything else into Domain/, stop and flag it rather than adding the
  import.
- New cross-layer dependencies must go through a protocol if the underlying
  implementation is something we've said we might replace (currently: CameraService,
  PoseDetectionService). Do not add new protocols for types that will only ever have
  one implementation — that's needless abstraction for this project's size.

## Dependency injection
- Manual, initializer-based injection only. No DI framework. New dependencies get wired
  in App/AppDependencies.swift.

## Third-party dependencies
- None allowed without explicit, separate approval. Default to Apple's native
  frameworks for everything. If you believe a third-party library is truly necessary,
  stop and explain why before adding it — do not add it silently.

## Error handling
- No force unwraps (`!`) except where genuinely provably safe with a comment explaining
  why (e.g. immediately after a guard that establishes the invariant). Prefer
  `guard let`/`if let`/`??` with sensible fallbacks.
- Camera/Vision failures should surface as typed errors, not silent no-ops — but should
  degrade gracefully in the UI (e.g. "camera unavailable" state) rather than crashing.

## Naming & structure
- No massive View files: if a SwiftUI View's body exceeds roughly 40-50 lines, extract
  subviews.
- Match file names to their primary type name.
- Keep PushUpThresholds.swift as the single source of truth for tunable constants — do
  not scatter magic numbers for angles/durations/confidence elsewhere in the codebase.

## Testing
- Any new logic added to Domain/ must ship with corresponding XCTest coverage in the
  same PR/step.
- Do not write tests that depend on a physical camera or Vision's actual ML output —
  those paths are verified manually on-device, not via automated tests.

## Performance
- Never call Vision's synchronous perform on the main thread.
- Never convert CVPixelBuffer to UIImage/CGImage unless there is a specific,
  named reason — Vision consumes CVPixelBuffer directly.
- Respect the project's frame-throttling design (~15 analyzed frames/sec) rather than
  running Vision on every camera frame.

## Comments
- Comment *why*, not *what*, especially around threshold values, coordinate-space
  conversions (Vision's normalized/bottom-left origin vs. SwiftUI's top-left), and any
  place mirroring (front camera) is handled.

## Scope discipline
- Do not add authentication, backend calls, CloudKit, push notifications, analytics
  SDKs, subscriptions/StoreKit, social features, multiple exercise types, or persistence
  beyond what's explicitly requested for the current step. If a step's prompt doesn't
  mention it, don't build it, even if it seems like an obvious next addition.

## Premature optimization
- Do not add caching, object pooling, or micro-optimizations beyond the explicit
  guidance already given (e.g. AsyncStream buffering policy, avoiding image-format
  conversions) unless a real, measured performance problem has been identified via
  the debug FPS readout.